<DocsLayout
  title="Persistent agents"
  eyebrow="Desktop"
  description="Create a paused project agent, preserve its mission and memory, and explicitly enable bounded proactive work while Roder Desktop is running."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/desktop/", title: "Roder Desktop" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/cloud-machines/", title: "Cloud Machines" }}
>
  <p>
    A persistent agent owns one saved Roder thread and a long-lived mission. It
    returns to that thread across check-ins and desktop restarts instead of starting
    a new conversation each time. Its editable memory carries decisions and next
    actions; native memory search and context compaction support longer work.
    Durable context does not mean an unlimited model context window.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide describes the desktop feature through <strong>v0.1.11</strong>.
    Persistent agents are distinct from short-lived subagents, runtime event
    subscriptions, and a cloud-hosted worker.
  </p>

  <h2>Create a project agent</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <strong>Persistent Agents</strong> in Roder Desktop.</li>
    <li>Choose an existing project workspace, a display name, and a concrete mission.</li>
    <li>Keep the engine's default model or choose a model and supported reasoning effort.</li>
    <li>Create the agent, then review its mission, editable memory, and run limits.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout">
    New agents start <strong>paused</strong>. Creating an agent creates its thread,
    but does not authorize a proactive conversation turn or enable scheduled work.
    Choose <strong>Run once</strong> for one explicit run, or separately enable
    proactive work after reviewing the controls.
  </div>

  <h2>Character generation is separate</h2>
  <p>
    Creation also queues a persona character through the installed Codex CLI's
    managed image-generation route. That job can use Codex quota even while the
    agent is paused. It requires native Codex sign-in, separate from Roder's
    inference-provider credentials, and does not enable proactive work.
  </p>
  <p>
    A failed or interrupted character job needs an explicit retry. Desktop does not
    silently fall back to a billed API request. The separate OpenAI API generation
    action requires its own key and explicit selection; saving the key alone does
    not generate an image. A character is presentation, not evidence that the
    persistent agent has started working.
  </p>

  <h2>Create one from chat</h2>
  <p>
    Desktop v0.1.10 introduced the built-in <code>$persistent-agent</code> skill and
    <code>persistent_agent_create</code> tool. For example, ask:
  </p>
  <pre><code>Create a persistent agent named "Docs caretaker" for this workspace.
Its mission is to keep the project's documentation aligned with released features.
Keep it paused.</code></pre>
  <p>
    The agent needs a name and mission. In the v0.1.11 interface,
    <code>workspaceId</code> is optional and defaults to the calling thread's existing
    workspace. To target a different workspace, select it in the UI or supply its
    existing workspace ID. An Issues project ID is not a workspace ID.
    Explicit model choices use <code>modelProvider</code> and <code>model</code>
    together; <code>reasoning</code> is optional.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tool does not enable proactive work. If the current thread does not
    advertise it, start a new desktop chat instead of treating a prose description
    as successful creation.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose when it can work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Run once:</strong> starts one manual run, including while the agent is paused.</li>
    <li><strong>Check-in interval:</strong> allows recurring checks after proactive work is enabled. Zero disables recurring checks.</li>
    <li><strong>Scheduled wake:</strong> stores a specific future check-in.</li>
    <li><strong>Engine event subscription:</strong> queues a check-in for selected forwarded notifications, such as <code>turn/completed</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Explicitly choose <strong>Enable proactive work</strong> to allow automatic runs.
    Intervals and pending wakes do nothing while paused. Repeated notifications
    coalesce, and events from the persistent agent's own thread are ignored.
    Only one active run may hold the agent's execution lease.
  </p>

  <h2>Set limits before enabling</h2>
  <p>
    The defaults are <strong>five run starts per rolling 24 hours</strong> and
    <strong>ten minutes per run</strong>. Manual runs consume the same run allowance.
    Time-limit enforcement checks periodically and requests interruption; it is not
    an instantaneous process kill. Approval and user-input waits remain subject to
    the run's time limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pause and interrupt</strong> saves the paused state and requests that an
    active run stop. The active lease remains until the engine confirms completion
    or an idle thread, preventing overlapping runs. Normal approval policy still
    applies to every action.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    A run allowance limits starts and wall-clock time, not dollars or exact token
    spending. Model calls, tools, and execution machines can incur separate costs.
    Manual compaction can also call a model outside the proactive run allowance.
    These controls are not a billing integration or hard financial cap.
  </div>

  <h2>Keep memory useful</h2>
  <p>
    Store the mission, current decisions, blockers, owned work, and next actions in
    concise memory. The agent's native tools can read or replace this memory with
    <code>persistent_agent_memory_read</code> and
    <code>persistent_agent_memory_write</code>. Additional retrievable facts use
    <code>persistent_agent_memory_save</code> and
    <code>persistent_agent_memory_query</code>. Scheduled wakes use
    <code>persistent_agent_wake_schedule</code> when advertised.
  </p>
  <p>
    Memory is local plaintext, not encrypted secret storage. Do not put credentials
    in it. The editable memory file is bounded to 32,000 characters; use native
    memory for additional facts rather than silently dropping older decisions.
    If an edit conflicts with an agent or external editor's change, reopen the
    agent and review the latest memory before saving.
  </p>

  <h2>What survives a restart</h2>
  <p>
    The mission, enabled state, memory, budget ledger, pending wakes, and thread
    identity are persisted locally. On restart, the service reconciles any active
    run with the engine before starting another. It does not replace a missing or
    failed thread with a fresh one to hide history or context limits.
  </p>
  <p>
    The desktop and its engine must be running for wakes to execute. No OS daemon,
    cloud worker, or closed-app scheduler is installed. Persisted due wakes are
    considered after restart; missed recurring intervals become one check-in rather
    than a backlog. Engine events emitted while the desktop is closed are not replayed.
  </p>

  <h2>Work across projects deliberately</h2>
  <p>
    A persistent agent is associated with a desktop workspace. A mission mentioning
    several repositories does not configure their access, synchronization, or
    monitoring. Give it the folders and tools it actually needs, and keep worker
    write ownership explicit. Delegation depends on the tools available to that
    thread; a mission cannot create unavailable subagent capabilities.
  </p>
  <p>
    Local Issues can link an organizational project to that workspace and its
    persistent agent without creating a second agent. Issue assignment still does
    not schedule work or automatically close an issue when a thread finishes.
    See <a href="/docs/desktop/">the desktop guide</a> for local issue workflows.
  </p>

  <h2>Source and interface scope</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop hosts the management route and
    <code>desktop/persistent-agents/*</code> methods; these are desktop additions,
    not a promise that every CLI or remote app-server exposes the same tools.
    See the tagged
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/electron/persistent-agents/service.ts">service implementation</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/electron/persistent-agents/creation-tool.ts">creation-tool contract</a>.
    Newer untagged workspace-selection or UI changes are outside this versioned guide.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
